#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124262 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "bf35c502-5a22-5953-9158-b2464d28c09f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3377" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,4⟩-⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟨1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+4xy),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+4xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6x+4y,\\,4x⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1) & =⟨6\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1,\\,4\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇g(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124263 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "dc1e4548-ce72-5618-86f4-b68b46b50550" :question/math-academy-id "e-3381" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot 2xy\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot {x}^{2}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(2,1) & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $- \\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124264 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/id #uuid "1eea4610-508a-5437-802e-9d009b777e82" :question/math-academy-id "e-3382" :question/problem "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right).$ What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})‖ & =‖⟨1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04dcdfb7-813c-5013-92a6-1f6a2e40d34a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562947} {:answer/id #uuid "3293ed3c-f84a-5bdd-b26a-c1ac19d7d55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563059} {:answer/id #uuid "6635b13b-ede2-5436-984d-23828cf04773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563181} {:answer/id #uuid "7d25f5da-9280-508e-a834-dd75fe932211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563220} {:answer/id #uuid "992d4136-ab07-5324-b78a-7177af5b18ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3293ed3c-f84a-5bdd-b26a-c1ac19d7d55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563059} :answer-field/id #uuid "235850d9-4b74-5c62-9d1d-41026c4c9828" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df6440e4-6b40-5b4e-87c9-62e58eb89d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130258" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{2}$ at the point $(-1,3)$ in the direction of the vector $v=i+j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2x-6y)\\,i+(2y-6x)j \\\\ ∇\\,f(-1,3) & =(2(-1)-6(3))\\,i+(2(3)-6(-1))j \\\\  & =-20\\,i+12\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,3) & =∇\\,f(-1,3)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(-20\\,i+12\\,j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{-20(1)+12(1)}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "053be929-7775-5fb0-bbbd-3704056bc94e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562948} {:answer/id #uuid "1e39c336-a544-5c66-93cf-3ffb2d7f86bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563011} {:answer/id #uuid "352ad72c-95f5-5fae-a15a-05da0c1da3cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563065} {:answer/id #uuid "53343afc-152e-5acb-9d54-08cb170116b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563133} {:answer/id #uuid "c90851b6-ccde-53db-beb3-491291ef5d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,2,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c90851b6-ccde-53db-beb3-491291ef5d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,2,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563388} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff3c854a-2ec4-5aac-9265-718757aee35e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8889b56a-bb69-56d7-b6a6-42f214a1a3f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-130270" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,0,-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,0,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x-yz,\\,2y-xz,\\,2z-xy⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,0,-2) & =⟨2(1)-0(-2),2(0)-1(-2),2(-2)-1(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2,2,-4⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4055cd-dc46-58cf-9414-ebd3aeced26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504052} {:answer/id #uuid "169a9454-865f-5c99-afad-24018fd51b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504072} {:answer/id #uuid "24013cd6-fd21-5ea4-a444-92864f2d2b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504094} {:answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} {:answer/id #uuid "2fefcbb7-1707-5b33-9ea0-368b42c461ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba947109-a6e8-5648-9e45-f1bf117b385d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93366598-9424-5753-a6aa-f85435a0ef2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130279" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5aa574d4-0537-5c0c-bc48-fbc415d1fec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563148} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6bd500-a363-5876-8fc0-e3190803b613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563153} {:answer/id #uuid "605a4f94-5783-5014-8c0c-c25bbcb39d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4e-3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563167} {:answer/id #uuid "83f43442-0622-5df7-8058-787b935ebf97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563240} {:answer/id #uuid "e79adf25-7969-5327-8be1-24d409ff0b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "605a4f94-5783-5014-8c0c-c25bbcb39d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4e-3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563167} :answer-field/id #uuid "75da4e92-2341-5f89-a517-37e979e4303c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fb365d6-3918-560a-af36-ae398310b595" :question/math-academy-id "q-130802" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)={y}^{x}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,e)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨4,-3⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,e)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,e).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{x}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{y}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,x{y}^{x-1}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,e) & =⟨{e}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡e,1{e}^{1-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨e,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,e)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,e) & =∇g(1,e)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨e,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨4,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{e(4)+1(-3)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4e-3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40cd1b3b-3226-567f-8df7-b4203af0e8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446186} {:answer/id #uuid "78161357-66de-58ad-955b-760220e2284c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446204} {:answer/id #uuid "7ced684c-4ebe-5a34-b02d-a8bda555a9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446205} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfd9ca6-8542-5ff2-910b-fbe85fdf3f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446207} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dd3ec0-5bd2-592f-a2b9-507da0e25254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ced684c-4ebe-5a34-b02d-a8bda555a9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446205} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6dc0783-a199-5ca8-ace2-ec2c865c730a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d39de67-7fd2-5aeb-a063-e9cf738816af" :question/math-academy-id "q-130806" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)={x}^{y}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{x}^{y-1},\\,{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,1) & =⟨(1){1}^{1-1},\\,({1}^{1})\\operatorname{ln}⁡1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $∇g(1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(1,1)‖ & =‖⟨1,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eb318ef-e5da-50bb-852d-abf0124a9413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446194} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1128a0-3bc8-5ee6-8690-02b7875e3982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446211} {:answer/id #uuid "94e3a4fd-f234-5419-b576-fa4fcdfcf2eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446213} {:answer/id #uuid "c41e1274-b58c-591c-ba2b-30404efa267c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446221} {:answer/id #uuid "c4350363-1f04-5bba-b31a-249136e6fc8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d1128a0-3bc8-5ee6-8690-02b7875e3982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446211} :answer-field/id #uuid "716b4932-e73e-59f5-9ec5-4b4ed730dece" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72e3df1a-6338-564c-adc2-85283b26953d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130811" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2\\sqrt{xy}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(8,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\sqrt{xy})i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{xy})j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\,j \\\\ -∇f(8,2) & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{8}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction of $-∇f(8,2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇f(8,2)‖ & =‖-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d2f5038-f42a-5fd7-a95d-a297da774c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446193} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d07651-85d5-5624-ae13-7a41e816c585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446216} {:answer/id #uuid "b248e7e5-413b-5466-b7ed-293308db0e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446218} {:answer/id #uuid "d058285d-919c-56d4-bc37-2e175c0f050a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446225} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4ac023-e663-5e98-b2d3-aa3c2ec67064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d058285d-919c-56d4-bc37-2e175c0f050a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446225} :answer-field/id #uuid "cea3cee4-a045-5dc7-8596-6cf26a0cb161" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c3374df-275b-5fb9-8864-40efeec8b5b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36306" :question/problem "Given the function  $g(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)^{2}$, find its directional derivative at the\npoint $A(-1,1)$ in the direction that's towards the point $B(3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨3,4⟩-⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}⟨4,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4x({x}^{2}+y),\\,2({x}^{2}+y)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1),2\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}+4\\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d46f12-0b75-50c9-a911-0b663e2a7dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi +e}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446179} {:answer/id #uuid "1683b5bd-9e3c-568d-b68d-d3cd665c9d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{4}+{e}^{2}}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446180} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ee910-194a-5db1-a61b-cb7e82e92ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446198} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7aff75-c59d-50b0-ace5-77c397f97e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446208} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe40dc8-4b41-5a6b-bd2a-d1a558dc2365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi +e}{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ee910-194a-5db1-a61b-cb7e82e92ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446198} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aca3088-e8f0-5dbc-95a5-523c9e4320d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8ea3ebc5-2c81-5696-aab4-931973a19233" :question/math-academy-id "q-37074" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(e,\\pi )$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(e,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(e,\\pi ) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}{e},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡e\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡e)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{e},\\,1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\cdot (-1)}{e},\\,1\\cdot (-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{e},\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(e,\\pi )$ in the direction of $∇g(e,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(e,\\pi )‖ & =‖⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{e},-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{{e}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c8383c9-5a00-5106-8ade-ebcb1afc1644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562966} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea52a0e-e6a5-522f-8a2f-e28cb7fa9518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563050} {:answer/id #uuid "48b88652-e6ed-5cdf-94e0-9c52630df3e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563105} {:answer/id #uuid "68b0f87b-d56a-5e56-bcb2-6c135e0e44a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563187} {:answer/id #uuid "804a3d7f-93d1-5460-9e8a-4cea93ac58d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ea52a0e-e6a5-522f-8a2f-e28cb7fa9518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563050} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f6b8a55-8b0f-5157-98ff-b7ec894bdaee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7872fd6-9c54-5e48-b08b-71d8ca0a2063" :question/math-academy-id "q-83552" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=2i+j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(2i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+\\frac{y}{x})\\,i+(\\frac{x}{y}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{1}{1})\\,i+(\\frac{1}{1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,f(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+j)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fb6012-956b-5c39-8935-74645f89ee82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}\\,i+2\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500483} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0516fc-fa33-5a32-bc85-021583caaccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500790} {:answer/id #uuid "a91d4692-51f1-5ef6-96a3-68a85bcad337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500817} {:answer/id #uuid "b20c5f55-306b-5cef-9a67-804b11be245c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500840} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c25011-b246-57b2-afb4-9949bd083e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i+\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b20c5f55-306b-5cef-9a67-804b11be245c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500840} :answer-field/id #uuid "941d1058-20a9-5c97-a5b7-7c5da913add0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2926681-2f02-501e-9c4c-db877ffeff07" :question/math-academy-id "q-83562" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\pi ,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2xy)\\text{ }i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(\\pi ,0) & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot 0)\\text{ }i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{\\pi }^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57ea209f-e155-526a-94ca-0d966c493e42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563141} {:answer/id #uuid "96c6d7cb-49e7-5e45-b566-508c8a442dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563281} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5e6c1e-a123-5805-9a48-ff55d4732a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563304} {:answer/id #uuid "b3791795-25ea-52db-9a06-ea2ad48e2ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563341} {:answer/id #uuid "d18ea431-dd2b-59ac-94dc-2a47eca46a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96c6d7cb-49e7-5e45-b566-508c8a442dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563281} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe69802c-24d6-5c9a-b43c-e07a25e5de2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ffacb628-f280-51e0-9811-c0f525cc08ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-83591" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+2{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+2{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+2{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+4x-\\frac{1}{x})i-x{sec}^{2}⁡y\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{1})i-1\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-4\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $-4\\,i-2\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00433fb2-2689-50b6-b5ae-b3213b484e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562937} {:answer/id #uuid "81f0d8bc-19f0-5e3d-ad6f-8e1c66829afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563232} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4239ca-8453-592c-a801-dc055bf9ad37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563360} {:answer/id #uuid "c97f5424-984d-5602-8981-349f5455aec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563389} {:answer/id #uuid "da9987fb-7331-55ac-8916-60a446822552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da9987fb-7331-55ac-8916-60a446822552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563423} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa2be9cd-8e25-5c22-a57c-dbf36efef798" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5075c32-f624-572f-a2d3-78616466d14e" :question/math-academy-id "q-86430" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=(2{x}^{2}-y)^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(-2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨-2,2⟩-⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-y)^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-y)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨16{x}^{3}-8xy,\\,2y-4{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-1,1) & =⟨16(-1)^{3}-8(-1)(1),\\,2(1)-4(-1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇f(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})-2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e252ff-bff2-5a0c-8722-678c22a7c16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517501} {:answer/id #uuid "070f81d7-bbdd-59f5-971a-c1e85fd656fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517503} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8dff85-9179-52c1-a1eb-e2b80b8d392e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517603} {:answer/id #uuid "636cdbc8-2c6a-553b-a040-1b1eb24c4aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517671} {:answer/id #uuid "9a89bbd5-05e0-5b9e-875f-8fbff4dcb441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a89bbd5-05e0-5b9e-875f-8fbff4dcb441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517783} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa6bc3a1-b754-5de6-a15a-17b5690dd1bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36bc1d86-9d95-5fad-8592-9df3687ca62a" :question/math-academy-id "q-86533" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,-1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,0⟩-⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨3,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8x(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}),\\,36{y}^{3}-24{x}^{2}y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1) & =⟨8(-1)\\cdot (2(-1)^{2}-3(-1)^{2}),36(-1)^{3}-24(-1)^{2}(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,-1) & =∇f(-1,-1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e8ca8f9-1d26-55d7-9b86-8c52d09b0ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517660} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ea4930-c661-5fcb-a591-ae748e162fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517804} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6eb5e3-7ae1-506a-bc7d-d480b4fe04c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517807} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a6152f-0b20-5bb6-96d5-09c1f86baab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517870} {:answer/id #uuid "eac115c2-4ed8-51a6-aa73-4f0569e0d56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac6eb5e3-7ae1-506a-bc7d-d480b4fe04c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517807} :answer-field/id #uuid "082380e4-f20f-513e-9d31-b936b5054d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d63b608-6564-54eb-94c2-8afc07416275" :question/math-academy-id "q-86540" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,-1,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,-\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,8z⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,-1,0) & =⟨\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,-\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,8\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "189ec891-a762-54dd-8e28-66f7d86f7621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563000} {:answer/id #uuid "a937d5cd-9309-5b00-a446-cdfd649551b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563326} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d80ddb-b0ea-50ab-81d2-6892d56e013c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563340} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1cc6cd-5009-53a5-9550-f7f07275d8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563398} {:answer/id #uuid "e69a9ba3-ccae-56d8-8e5f-5d06921f70e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd1cc6cd-5009-53a5-9550-f7f07275d8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563398} :answer-field/id #uuid "45ab83aa-a438-50c5-87d1-85ddbe0b15e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "56172e38-6320-5f34-8b75-aab1067f37e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-86543" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,\\frac{2}{y}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4) & =⟨-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,\\frac{2}{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ in the direction of $∇g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)‖ & =‖⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
